{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי לא דבר רק הוא", :translit "ki lo davar rek hu", :gloss "For it is not an empty thing it"} {:he "תגין על הקו\"ף", :translit "tagin al ha-kuf", :gloss "Tagin on the qof"} {:he "תילי תילים של הלכות", :translit "tilei tilim shel halakhot", :gloss "heaps upon heaps of halakhot"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Tagin drawn on the letter qof in the word רק"}], :frame {:text "The verse states that Torah is not an empty matter (Deuteronomy 32:47). The Ba'al HaTurim reads the decorative crowns (tagin) on the letter qof as carrying their own legal content, reinforcing that every element of the written Torah, down to the strokes of individual letters, yields halakhic teaching.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 32:47"]}} {:quotes [{:he "רק", :translit "rek", :gloss "empty"} {:he "והבור רק", :translit "veha-bor rek", :gloss "and the pit was empty"} {:he "כמו שהבור ריק ואינו מתמלא מחולייתו כן התורה לא יוכל אדם להתמלאות ממנה", :translit "kemo sheha-bor reik ve-eino mitmalei me-cholyato ken ha-torah lo yukhal adam lehitmaleot mimenah", :gloss "just as a pit is empty and not filled from its lining, so the Torah — a person cannot be filled from it"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["והבור רק" "רק"]}], :frame {:text "The word רק appears twice in the Masorah. The parallel occurrence is והבור רק (Genesis 37:24), describing the pit into which Joseph was cast. The Midrash reads that pit as empty of water but full of snakes and scorpions (Shabbat 22a), establishing the pit as a symbol of something structurally hollow. The Ba'al HaTurim links the two verses: just as a pit cannot be filled from its own earthen walls, so a person can never be fully filled from Torah.", :sources ["Genesis 37:24" "Shabbat 22a" "Deuteronomy 32:47"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Tagin drawn on the letter qof in the word רק", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["והבור רק" "רק"], :by :selah, :located [